</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Per-frame latency measurement</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We measure two types of latency: waveform-level and token-level latency.
Waveform-level latency is the elapsed time from feeding the previous audio token <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> into the model until the output <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is incrementally decoded into a waveform, including the encoding of a raw video frame <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Token<span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>level latency is identical to this but excludes the waveform decoding (Detailed in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS5" title="B.5 Per-frame Latency Measurement ‣ Appendix B Experimental Details ‣ SoundReactor: Frame-level online video-to-audio generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a>).
We perform this benchmark on a single H100 GPU with a batch size of one, using a single CUDA stream.
We provide the mean and standard deviation over the <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> clips (<math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math>FPS, <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="480" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>480</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">480</annotation></semantics></math>p, <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> second) with the default our ECT at <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega=3</annotation></semantics></math>.
As demonstrated in Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Generation beyond training context window ‣ 4.4 Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ SoundReactor: Frame-level online video-to-audio generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, our model achieves both remarkably low per-frame token-level and waveform-level latency under NFE=1 to NFE=4 on the head.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Ablation Study</h3>

<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02110v1/ect_ablation_mmd_center_2.svg" id="S4.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/399;" width="548" height="399"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((a)) </span>MMD<math id="S4.F4.sf1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{CLAP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>CLAP</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{CLAP}}</annotation></semantics></math>(Center)<math id="S4.F4.sf1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02110v1/ect_abl_ol3_center_step.svg" id="S4.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/413;" width="548" height="413"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((b)) </span>MMD<math id="S4.F4.sf2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{OpenL3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>OpenL3</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{OpenL3}}</annotation></semantics></math>(Center)<math id="S4.F4.sf2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02110v1/ect_ablation_kl.svg" id="S4.F4.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/395;" width="548" height="395"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((c)) </span>KL<math id="S4.F4.sf3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\text{PaSST}}\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>PaSST</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{PaSST}}\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02110v1/ect_ablation_IB.svg" id="S4.F4.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/399;" width="548" height="399"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((d)) </span><math id="S4.F4.sf4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{IB-Score}\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>IB-Score</mtext><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{IB-Score}\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Ablation study on ECT. <span id="S4.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">CF</span> and <span id="S4.F4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">IN</span> indicate different mapping functions (see Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Training Details ‣ Appendix B Experimental Details ‣ SoundReactor: Frame-level online video-to-audio generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>). <math id="S4.F4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2</annotation></semantics></math> denote dropout rates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">Srivastava et al., 2014</a>)</cite> during finetuning. <span id="S4.F4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">CF</span>–<math id="S4.F4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2</annotation></semantics></math> is our default.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we ablate two factors in ECT that crucially affect sample quality: i) the mapping function in Eq (<a href="#S3.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Stage 2: Consistency fine-tuning via ECT ‣ 3.3 Training Framework ‣ 3 SoundReactor ‣ SoundReactor: Frame-level online video-to-audio generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) and ii) the network dropout rate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">Srivastava et al., 2014</a>)</cite>.
We use two representative choices from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geng et al. (2025b)</a></cite>, denoted <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IN</span> and <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CF</span> (detailed in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Training Details ‣ Appendix B Experimental Details ‣ SoundReactor: Frame-level online video-to-audio generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>). Under <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CF</span>, we test dropout rates of <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CF</span>–<math id="S4.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2</annotation></semantics></math> is identical to our default ECT.).
As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.5 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ SoundReactor: Frame-level online video-to-audio generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, comparing <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CF</span>–<math id="S4.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math> and <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CF</span>–<math id="S4.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2</annotation></semantics></math> reveals that the dropout rate substantially impacts sample quality.
This aligns with prior findings in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song &amp; Dhariwal (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geng et al. (2025b)</a></cite> and newly shows that tuning it remains significantly important even in MAR<span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text">-</span>style models. Comparing <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IN</span>–<math id="S4.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2</annotation></semantics></math> and <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CF</span>–<math id="S4.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2</annotation></semantics></math>, both settings show comparable performance on NFE=2 and 4. Notably, however, only <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CF</span>–<math id="S4.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2</annotation></semantics></math> can generate high-quality samples with NFE=1. For a deeper understanding of our model, we provide extensive ablations on various aspects of both the generator (in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Ablation Study on Generator Training ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiments ‣ SoundReactor: Frame-level online video-to-audio generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a> and <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Ablation Study on Sampling Strategy ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiments ‣ SoundReactor: Frame-level online video-to-audio generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>) and vision conditioning (in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Ablation Study on Vision Conditioning ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiments ‣ SoundReactor: Frame-level online video-to-audio generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>), which demonstrate that our design choices contribute to the overall performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we introduce the novel task of frame-level online V2A generation and propose SoundReactor, the first simple yet effective framework tailored for this setting.
Our experiments demonstrate that SoundReactor achieves high-quality full-band stereo audio generation under the end-to-end causal constraint while maintaining low per-frame latency.
Extensive ablations provide key insights into the respective contributions of our design choices within our framework.
We anticipate this work will motivate the research community on sound generative models to explore interactive multimodal applications, providing a foundational component for live content creation and sounding world models.</p>
</div>
